# Original topic guides for the 8 short Digistore pages. General guidance only; product facts stay as on the vendor page.
D = {}
D['self-care-blueprint'] = dict(t='How to build a self-care routine when you are busy', s=[
('Start with what you actually need', 'Busy people often copy someone else\'s self-care list and then feel guilty when it does not fit. A better first step is a quick audit: where do you feel most depleted right now, your body, your mind, your relationships or your time? Your routine should start there.'),
('Use tiny, fixed slots', 'You probably do not have an hour a day. Most people do have three 10-minute gaps: before everyone wakes, a lunch break, and the time after the kids or work wind down. Pick one gap and give it one self-care action, every day.'),
('Pair self-care with things you already do', 'Stretch while the kettle boils. Listen to a calm podcast on the commute. Do your skincare as the signal that the workday is over. Pairing removes the need for extra willpower.'),
('Protect your energy, not just your time', 'Some self-care is about subtracting: saying no to one optional commitment, muting a group chat, leaving a task undone. Less drain can matter as much as more rest.'),
('Plan a weekly anchor', 'One longer block a week, such as a Sunday bath, a walk with a friend or a solo coffee, gives you something to look forward to and a moment to reset.'),
('Review and simplify', 'Every few weeks, drop what you are not doing and keep what helps. A short routine you keep is better than a long one you abandon.')],
faq=[('Is self-care selfish if I have a family?', 'No. Looking after your own health and energy helps you show up better for the people who depend on you.'),('How much time does a self-care routine need?', 'Even 10 to 15 minutes a day can help if it is consistent.'),('Is this ebook medical advice?', 'No. For ongoing stress, health or mood concerns, talk to a professional.')])
D['self-care-is-not-selfish'] = dict(t='Why self-care is not selfish (and how to rest without guilt)', s=[
('Where the guilt comes from', 'Many women grow up learning that being needed is the same as being good. Resting can then feel like letting people down. Naming that belief is the first step to questioning it.'),
('Rest is maintenance, not a reward', 'You do not need to earn rest by finishing everything first, because "everything" is never finished. Treat rest like charging your phone: it is what makes the next part of the day possible.'),
('Start with small boundaries', 'Saying no to one small request a week, or protecting one evening, builds the muscle gently. The guilt is often loudest at the start and fades as people adjust.'),
('Notice your energy signals', 'Irritability, trouble sleeping and dreading simple tasks are often signs you have been running on empty. Treat them as signals to rest earlier, not proof you are failing.'),
('Build daily rituals', 'A morning cup of tea in silence, a short walk, five minutes of journaling: small rituals give you a regular, guilt-free moment that belongs to you.'),
('Ask for help', 'Sharing the load, whether chores, childcare or work tasks, is part of self-care too. People often help when asked clearly.')],
faq=[('How do I stop feeling guilty for resting?', 'Start small, remind yourself rest is maintenance, and notice that the people around you usually cope fine.'),('Is this a printable?', 'No, it is an ebook.'),('Is this therapy?', 'No. If guilt or exhaustion feel overwhelming, please talk to a professional.')])
D['healing-through-self-love'] = dict(t='Self-love journal prompts: how to start and go deeper', s=[
('What self-love journaling is', 'Self-love journaling is writing that helps you notice how you speak to yourself, what you value and what you need. It is less about positive thinking and more about honest, kind attention.'),
('Start with gentle prompts', 'Begin with easy questions: What did I do well today? What made me feel calm this week? What do I like about how I treat others? Gentle prompts build the habit before harder questions.'),
('Notice your inner voice', 'Write down one critical thought you had today. Then write how you would answer a friend who said that about themselves. The gap between the two is useful information.'),
('Explore values and needs', 'Prompts like "What matters most to me?" and "What do I need more of?" help you make choices that fit your life rather than other people\'s expectations.'),
('Use exercises, not only prompts', 'Workbook-style exercises such as letters to your younger self, lists of strengths or a boundaries map turn reflection into action.'),
('Go at your own pace', 'Some prompts may bring up hard feelings. It is fine to skip them or come back later. If something feels too heavy, reach out to someone you trust or a professional.')],
faq=[('Do I need journaling experience?', 'No. The vendor describes it as suitable whether you are new to self-love work or building on it.'),('Is it a workbook?', 'Yes, it includes guided workbook exercises with each lesson, per the vendor.'),('Is it therapy?', 'No. It is a self-help ebook and workbook.')])
D['cozy-fall-winter-habit-planner'] = dict(t='How to plan a cozy fall and winter glow up', s=[
('Why a seasonal planner works', 'A full-year planner can feel like a big commitment. A seasonal planner covers just the cold months, when routines most often slip and when many people want a reset before the new year.'),
('Set one monthly goal', 'Pick one main priority per month, such as "sleep earlier", "read more" or "move daily", and break it into two or three action steps.'),
('Plan the week, including meals', 'Weekly pages with a meal plan and a water tracker make it easier to eat warm, regular meals and stay hydrated when the heating dries the air.'),
('Track a few cozy habits', 'Reading, gentle exercise, hydration and self-care are good starting habits for winter. Track them for 30 days and see what sticks.'),
('Reflect monthly', 'A short monthly review of wins and a gratitude list helps you notice good moments in darker months.'),
('Keep it light', 'Print only the pages you use. The goal is a cozier, calmer season, not another to-do list.')],
faq=[('Is it editable?', 'The vendor says it includes a link to an editable Canva template as well as printable PDFs.'),('What sizes?', 'A4 and US Letter, per the order form.'),('Is it dated?', 'The cover has a 2026-2027 cozy theme, per the vendor.')])
D['90-day-goal-planner'] = dict(t='How to plan your next 90 days (a simple goal framework)', s=[
('Why 90 days', 'A year is long enough to procrastinate; a week is too short to change much. Ninety days, about one quarter, is long enough for real progress and short enough to stay focused.'),
('Choose one to three goals', 'More goals mean less progress on each. Choose up to three and make them specific and measurable, for example "save $600" rather than "save money".'),
('Break goals into monthly milestones', 'Split each goal into three monthly milestones, then into weekly actions. Weekly actions are what you actually schedule.'),
('Plan weekly, check daily', 'Each week, choose the actions that move your goals forward and put them in your calendar. Each day, check off what you did. Small daily wins keep motivation up.'),
('Handle procrastination early', 'If a task keeps slipping, make it smaller or schedule it at your best-energy time. Starting for five minutes is often enough to keep going.'),
('Review at the end', 'At day 90, review what worked, celebrate wins and decide the next quarter\'s goals based on what you learned.')],
faq=[('Is this a printed planner?', 'No. It is an ebook, though the vendor says it includes daily, weekly and monthly planning pages.'),('What is SMART?', 'Specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound goals.'),('Refund policy?', 'The vendor states a 60-day money-back guarantee.')])
D['organized-life-blueprint'] = dict(t='How to declutter and organize your home, time and digital life', s=[
('Start with one small space', 'Decluttering a whole home at once is overwhelming. Start with a drawer, a shelf or your bag. A quick visible win builds momentum.'),
('Use keep, donate, toss', 'Sort items into three piles and decide quickly. If you have not used something in a year and it has no strong meaning, it is probably safe to let go.'),
('Give everything a home', 'Clutter often comes back because things have no fixed place. Once each item has a home, tidying takes minutes.'),
('Organize your time', 'Write all tasks in one list, then pick the top three for each day. A weekly review keeps the list from growing out of control.'),
('Clear your digital clutter', 'Unsubscribe from emails you never read, delete unused apps, create simple folders for files and back up your photos. Digital clutter drains attention too.'),
('Build daily habits', 'Ten minutes of tidying each evening, a clear inbox once a day and a weekly planning session keep systems working long term.')],
faq=[('Where should I start decluttering?', 'Somewhere small and visible, like a drawer or counter, for a quick win.'),('Does it cover digital organization?', 'Yes, the vendor lists tips for email, files and devices.'),('Format?', 'An ebook, delivered digitally.')])
D['overcoming-self-doubt'] = dict(t='How to overcome self-doubt and answer your inner critic', s=[
('Notice the inner critic', 'Self-doubt often sounds like a fact: "I am not good enough", "I will fail". Writing these thoughts down helps you see them as thoughts, not truths.'),
('Ask for the evidence', 'For each doubt, list evidence for and against it. Most of the time, the evidence against is stronger than it felt in the moment.'),
('Talk to yourself like a friend', 'If a friend said the same thing about themselves, what would you tell them? Use that kinder voice with yourself.'),
('Build self-trust through small promises', 'Keep small promises to yourself, like a 10-minute walk or finishing one task. Each kept promise is evidence that you can rely on yourself.'),
('Expect setbacks', 'Setbacks are part of growth, not proof that the doubt was right. Ask what you learned and what you will try next.'),
('Get support when needed', 'If self-doubt is constant or linked to low mood or anxiety, talking to a counsellor or therapist can help.')],
faq=[('Can self-doubt be overcome?', 'Many people learn to manage it with practice, such as challenging thoughts and building self-trust.'),('Is this ebook therapy?', 'No. It is a self-help ebook.'),('Refund policy?', 'The vendor states a 60-day money-back guarantee.')])
D['art-of-saying-no'] = dict(t='How to say no without guilt: a practical guide', s=[
('Why saying no is hard', 'Many people fear that saying no will disappoint others or damage relationships. In practice, clear and kind boundaries often make relationships more honest and less resentful.'),
('Pause before you answer', 'You do not have to answer straight away. "Let me check and get back to you" gives you time to decide what you actually want.'),
('Keep it short and kind', 'A long explanation invites negotiation. Try: "Thank you for thinking of me, but I can\'t this time." You do not need to give a reason.'),
('Offer an alternative only if you want to', '"I can\'t help this week, but I could next month" is useful when you genuinely want to help later. It is optional.'),
('Expect some pushback', 'Some people will test a new boundary. Repeat your answer calmly, without new reasons. Consistency is what makes boundaries stick.'),
('Start with low-stakes requests', 'Practise on small things first, like an optional meeting or an extra favour. Each easy no makes the harder ones feel more possible.')],
faq=[('How do I say no politely?', 'Thank the person, say no clearly and briefly, and skip long explanations.'),('Does the ebook include scripts?', 'The vendor says it includes scripts for pushy bosses and demanding family members.'),('Is this legal or psychological advice?', 'No.')])
